  "answer_markdown": "When you retry failed records in a batch run using `retry_failed_records()`, the following happens to the run's evaluators and results in the console:\n\n1. The SDK identifies and reprocesses only the records with status `\"error\"` (failed generation or scoring) [1].\n\n2. The **original run's evaluators** (including run-level evaluators) are **recomputed** with the updated data after the failed records are retried and patched [1].\n   - If the original run had `run_evaluators`, their scores are recalculated based on the successfully processed records (including the newly patched ones) [1].\n   - Run-level evaluators execute **after all records have been processed and scored**, even if some records initially failed [2].\n\n3. The **original run in Studio is updated in place**—no duplicate runs are created, and no manual cleanup is required [1].\n   - The console reflects the **patched results**, combining the original successful records with the newly retried ones.\n\n4. The `retry_failed_records()` method returns a result object showing how many records were retried (`retried_count`) and how many were successfully patched (`patched_count`) [1].\n\n### Example Workflow\n```python\nresult = await client.evaluation.retry_failed_records(\n    run_id=run.run_id,\n    dataset=dataset,\n    task=task,\n    evaluators=[Evaluator(name=\"accuracy\", scorer=scorer)],\n    system=system,\n)\nprint(f\"Retried: {result.retried_count}, Patched: {result.patched_count}\")\n```\n\nThis ensures the run's evaluators and results remain consistent and up-to-date without requiring a full rerun [1].",
